This session made me think differently about the role of a Product Manager.
Earlier, I mostly looked at a PM as someone who connects business and technology teams.
The discussion made me realise that a PM is actually the owner of the product and its outcome.
Even when different teams execute the work, the PM still has to take responsibility for the final result.
The idea of “influence without authority” was something I could relate to from my own work experience.
Many times, we need to get things done through different teams without directly managing them.
So, communication and influencing people become as important as technical knowledge.
I also liked the discussion around trade-offs.
In real projects, we cannot always choose the technically perfect option.
Sometimes the second-best option may make more sense because of cost, resources, skills or future flexibility.
This is something I have experienced in automation projects as well.

Things like data, model behaviour, feedback loops and AI uncertainty become part of the PM's responsibility.
The discussion also made me think about how AI can change the way we build products, not just the product itself.
For me, the biggest takeaway was to focus less on completing activities and more on achieving the intended outcome.
I also realised that being accountable for a product means being comfortable with making difficult decisions.
Overall, this session gave me a more realistic understanding of what a PM actually does in practice.
My key takeaway is: a PM may not do every task, but ultimately owns the problem, the decisions and the outcome.
